- The Last Supper is important partly because of its connection to the Jewish Passover (see {{Exodus 12:1-51|Exodus 12}}). 

The steps of the Jewish liturgy for Passover are as follows: 

- Festal blessing;

- first cup (see {{Luke 22:17-18|Luke 22:17–18}});

- eating bitter herbs (see {{Matthew 26:23|Matt 26:23}} and corresponding study note);

- eating unleavened bread ({{Matthew 26:26|26:26}}; {{Mark 14:22|Mark 14:22}});

- mixing the second cup; 

- reading the Passover *haggadah *(this is a retelling of the Exodus story, {{Exodus 12:1-51|Exodus 12}}); 

- first part of the *hallel* (songs of praise, {{Psalm 113:1-114:8|Psalms 113–114}}; see study note on Psalms 113–118);

- drinking the second cup; 

- blessing and eating unleavened bread ({{Matthew 26:26|Matt 26:26}}; {{Mark 14:22|Mark 14:22}}; {{Luke 22:19|Luke 22:19}}); 

- eating the Passover lamb; 

- third cup ({{Matthew 26:27-28|Matt 26:27–28}}; {{Mark 14:23-24|Mark 14:23–24}}; {{Luke 22:20|Luke 22:20}}); 

- fourth cup (see {{Matthew 26:29|Matt 26:29}}[?]; {{Mark 14:25|Mark 14:25}}[?]); 

- time for fellowship; 

- second part of the *hallel* ({{Psalm 115:1-118:29|Psalms 115–118}}; see {{Matthew 26:30|Matthew 26:30}}). 

The New Testament stories of the Last Supper do not include every part of a typical Jewish Passover. Yet, they include the key parts that gave meaning to the early Christian celebration of the Lord’s Supper or Eucharist (see {{1 Corinthians 11:23-25|1 Corinthians 11:23–25}}). Jesus, the Lamb of God, is the ultimate Passover lamb and the perfect sacrifice for sin. 

- *this is My body … My blood:* Ever since the Protestant Reformation, there have been three main interpretations of the words of Jesus about the bread and wine: 

- Transubstantiation: The bread and wine are believed to change into the actual body and blood of Christ, while still appearing to be bread and wine.

- Real Presence: Christ is understood to be truly present in the sacrament in a mysterious but real way that is beyond human understanding.

- Memorial or Symbolic View: The bread and wine are seen as symbols. The act of sharing them recalls Christ’s death and resurrection, and believers receive spiritual benefit through faith.

The Gospels describe Jesus having five legal hearings: 

- an informal hearing with Annas ({{John 18:13-24|John 18:13–24}});

- a night hearing with the Sanhedrin ({{Matthew 26:59-66|Matthew 26:59–66}}); 

- a morning hearing with the Sanhedrin ({{Matthew 27:1-2|Matthew 27:1–2}}); 

- a hearing with Herod Antipas ({{Luke 23:6-12|Luke 23:6–12}}); and

- a two-part trial with Pilate ({{Matthew 27:11-31|Matthew 27:11–31}}; see {{Luke 23:6-12|Luke 23:6–12}}). 

These hearings form two main trials, a Jewish trial and a Roman trial.
